"""Item-specific contrasts for distractors whose wording needs more than a service definition."""
NOTES={
3:{'a':'An assumed role supplies temporary credentials; persistent credentials belong to a different credential model.','c':'Route-table entries determine network packet paths. IAM roles define assumable permissions and credentials, not those network routes.','d':'Billing reports describe usage and charges. Enabling them is not the defining purpose of an IAM role.'},
9:{'a':'WAF filters HTTP requests, including application attacks. Built-in AWS DDoS protection is the Shield Standard tier.','c':'Config evaluates resource configurations; it is not the DDoS mitigation layer.','d':'VPN creates encrypted tunnels; it is not automatic DDoS protection for CloudFront and Route 53.'},
21:{'a':'Security groups filter allowed connections; they do not impose a metered amount of transferred data.','b':'Encryption keys are managed through cryptographic services such as KMS, not network interface rules.','d':'Scaling policies change capacity; a security group only controls allowed traffic.'},
58:{'b':'CloudTrail records API events, not packet flows. VPC Flow Logs provide supported network-flow metadata.','c':'CloudTrail audit events do not encrypt the application resources being audited.','d':'Key management is the role of KMS or other cryptographic services, not CloudTrail event logging.'},
105:{'a':'Multiple AZs improve resilience within a Region; they do not inherently move an application geographically closer to global users.','b':'Cross-AZ traffic can add transfer charges, so using more AZs is not an inherent data-transfer saving.','d':'Adding failure domains does not directly increase the storage capacity of an existing service.'},
117:{'a':'Auto Scaling changes compute capacity; VPC/subnet/routing configuration is a separate networking task.','c':'Instance count does not establish a cross-Region database consistency model.','d':'Compute scaling does not encrypt stored data.'},
119:{'a':'Spot is spare interruptible capacity, not a dedicated-host purchase model.','c':'Spot reclamation can interrupt instances; Spot alone is not database high availability.','d':'Spot does not guarantee uninterrupted or consistently available web server capacity.'},
124:{'a':'A synchronous standby is an availability mechanism, not a general storage-throughput upgrade.','b':'Backup behavior may differ, but faster backups are not the primary reason to select Multi-AZ.','d':'Cross-AZ redundancy can add costs; it is not a data-transfer cost-reduction feature.'},
138:{'a':'A global replica table is an active replica, not a historical backup recovery point.','c':'Local regional reads can help latency, but faster arbitrary queries are not the defining global-table replication feature.','d':'Capacity auto scaling and regional replication are separate table features.'},
144:{'a':'Some load balancers support TLS and security integrations, but distributing incoming requests is the primary load-balancing benefit.','b':'Request balancing does not replicate application database records.','d':'Load balancing is not a general guarantee of reduced data-transfer charges.'},
165:{'b':'Lambda logs are stored through separate logging integrations; log persistence is not its principal serverless execution advantage.','c':'The serverless model avoids customer-managed dedicated instance capacity.','d':'Lambda execution does not administer the application database.'},
175:{'a':'The launch definition controls how instances are created, not the metric-based capacity decision.','c':'Scheduled actions follow a timetable; the clarified question asks for demand-metric-based behavior.','d':'Instance refresh rolls replacements to update instances rather than deciding demand-driven fleet size.'},
182:{'b':'Instance size can affect performance, but dedicated instance types do not explain Aurora standby failover.','c':'Serverless capacity and other configured scaling features are distinct from database failover.','d':'Security telemetry detects issues; it is not the replicated database availability mechanism.'},
188:{'b':'Beanstalk supports different tenancy/configuration choices, but dedicated instances are not its defining application lifecycle function.','c':'A database backup policy concerns data recovery; it is not the application code deployment lifecycle.','d':'Security settings are important environment configuration, not the main deployment/version-management function.'},
192:{'b':'Some load balancers terminate TLS, but encrypting the client path is an additional feature rather than the primary request-distribution role.','c':'Load balancers do not create EC2 snapshots.','d':'A load balancer does not install or resolve application software dependencies.'},
196:{'a':'Global Database adds optional cross-Region replication; it is not required for Aurora regional storage redundancy.','b':'Changing compute capacity does not by itself explain storage durability and standby recovery.','d':'Network security restricts access but does not implement Aurora storage replication.'},
202:{'a':'Read replicas offload reads; primary writes still go to the writer.','b':'Replicas can assist recovery, but read performance is their main distinguishing purpose; synchronous standby HA is a different deployment.','d':'Replicas are not primarily a data-access or encryption security feature.'},
208:{'a':'CloudFront is a delivery service, not the feature that encrypts the application database or source object at rest.','b':'Durability of authoritative content is primarily a storage-layer concern.','d':'A CDN does not provide arbitrary analytics or data transformation simply by delivering content.'},
212:{'a':'Global Accelerator can add charges; universal cost reduction is not its defining capability.','b':'Monitoring simplicity does not explain optimized global network routing.','d':'Its main distinction is global endpoint routing/performance rather than a replacement for identity or data security.'},
217:{'a':'Transfer Acceleration does not lower the storage-class rate and can add transfer charges.','c':'Transfer Acceleration changes the network path; stored-object encryption is configured independently.','d':'Acceleration does not compute analytics over the transferred data.'},
223:{'a':'Geolocation routing chooses based on user location categories; latency routing uses measured latency to configured Regions.','b':'Equal/weighted traffic distribution is not the definition of latency routing.','d':'Failover routing selects a recovery endpoint by primary/secondary health state rather than primarily by latency.'},
236:{'a':'CloudWatch metrics and logs observe the workload; Auto Scaling acts on configured capacity policies.','c':'An EC2 Auto Scaling group is regional; multi-Region application deployment requires separate infrastructure and routing.','d':'Infrastructure templates or management tools define resource configuration; scaling adjusts group capacity.'},
240:{'a':'Log ingestion/storage is provided by CloudWatch Logs; Insights is the query/analysis capability.','c':'Exporting or backing up logs to S3 is separate from querying them with Insights.','d':'Query results can be visualized, but the principal Insights function is log analysis rather than only dashboard presentation.'},
251:{'a':'An in-memory cache is not a low-cost long-duration archive.','c':'A cache may support persistence features, but authoritative backup and restore is not its main caching use case.','d':'A warehouse provides durable analytical SQL storage/processing; a cache targets low-latency memory access.'},
254:{'a':'Equal distribution occurs only when the configured weights are equal; arbitrary relative weights are supported.','b':'Health checks can affect eligible responses, but weighting is the selection rule named here.','d':'Primary/secondary failover is a separate routing policy.'},
264:{'a':'Caching primarily offloads repeated reads; it does not inherently increase the relational primary write capacity.','c':'Cache lookups do not create RDS backup recovery points.','d':'Transient hot-data caching is not long-term archival storage.'},
267:{'a':'Optional archive tiers can be enabled, but Intelligent-Tiering does not automatically archive every object by default.','c':'Access tier optimization is not private-cloud storage or IAM/network isolation.','d':'Lifecycle rules perform configured transitions/expiry; access-based movement inside Intelligent-Tiering is the distinguishing mechanism.'},
269:{'a':'Logs help observe behavior but do not themselves supply replacement capacity or request distribution.','c':'Encryption protects data and is separate from elastic compute capacity.','d':'Backups restore earlier state, rather than managing live request capacity.'},
273:{'a':'Read replicas do not add primary write throughput.','c':'Backup retention preserves historical recovery points; it is distinct from a running read-serving replica.','d':'Supported cross-Region replicas are optional; improved read scalability is the general purpose and replication need not cross Regions.'},
277:{'b':'Global Accelerator uses network-level endpoint routing; Route 53 is the DNS policy service.','c':'Global Accelerator requires existing deployed endpoints; it does not deploy applications.','d':'Resource performance monitoring is a separate telemetry function, not its main global-network role.'},
281:{'b':'Monitoring reports workload state; ELB directly distributes traffic.','c':'Auto Scaling controls instance count; ELB controls request distribution among registered targets.','d':'Deployment tooling updates code and infrastructure; load balancing is request routing.'},
283:{'a':'With Fargate, AWS manages worker hosts; the customer manages tasks and application configuration.','b':'ECS service scaling policies adjust application task capacity; Fargate alone is not the entire scaling policy.','d':'Network traffic still needs deliberate design; Fargate is primarily a managed execution-capacity model.'},
287:{'b':'Container service orchestration is provided by ECS/EKS, while Lambda primarily executes function invocations.','c':'Persist data in a suitable storage service; function execution is not the authoritative storage layer.','d':'A full web application can use Lambda but also requires API, state and other components; event-driven code is its direct primary use.'},
293:{'b':'Performance monitoring records metrics/logs; WAF filters supported web requests.','c':'Content delivery is CloudFront/CDN functionality; WAF may integrate with it but does not perform caching.','d':'Backup and recovery preserve application data, not malicious request filtering.'},
318:{'b':'Spot capacity is reclaimable, so it cannot guarantee continued availability.','c':'Reserved Instances and Savings Plans provide commitment discounts; Spot does not require that long-term baseline commitment.','d':'The chosen instance type determines hardware resources; Spot pricing alone does not provide more capacity per instance.'},
}
